<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Godwin, Earl of Wessex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1001–1053</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Godwin was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> nobleman who rose under Cnut to become Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and the most powerful man in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after the king. His daughter Edith married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>, but a quarrel with the king led to the exile of Godwin's family in 1051 until their armed return in 1052. His son Harold succeeded to his earldom and became king in 1066.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Godwin's father was probably Wulfnoth Cild, a thegn of Sussex whose origin is unknown. In 1009 Wulfnoth was accused of unknown crimes at a muster of the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a> and fled with twenty ships; the ships sent to pursue him were destroyed in a storm. Godwin was probably an adherent of Æthelred's eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>, who left him an estate at Compton, Sussex. After Cnut seized the throne in 1016, Godwin's rise was rapid. By 1018 he was an earl, probably of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, and by about 1020 of all Wessex. He accompanied Cnut on an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> between 1019 and 1023, distinguished himself there, and married Gytha, sister of the Danish earl Ulf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Cnut died in 1035, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was disputed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-harefoot">Harold Harefoot</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a>, and Godwin supported Harthacnut. In 1036 Alfred Ætheling, younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a> and Æthelred, attempted an invasion of England; Godwin intercepted him and handed him to Harold Harefoot. Alfred was blinded and died soon afterwards. Godwin's responsibility was disputed, but the affair left a stain that affected his future. He almost certainly supported Harold's recognition as king in 1037, backed Harthacnut's accession in 1040 and, after 1042, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>.</p><p>Soon after Edward became king he extended Godwin's jurisdiction to include Kent, and in January 1045 Godwin secured his daughter Edith's marriage to the king. Godwin led opposition as Edward drew advisers from a Normano-French circle. After a violent clash between the people of Dover and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eustace-ii-count-of-boulogne">Eustace II, Count of Boulogne</a>, Godwin refused the king's order to punish Dover. Edward and Godwin raised armies, and an agreement was reached to bring the case before a court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Losing support, Godwin and his family fled, he himself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and his sons Leofwine and Harold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. They returned in spring 1052 with armed forces and the support of the navy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and peasants, which compelled Edward to restore the earldom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Godwin died on 15 April 1053, days after collapsing at a royal banquet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>; contemporary accounts suggest a sudden illness, possibly a stroke. A later story by Aelred of Rievaulx, which appears to be Norman propaganda, has him choking on a crust after swearing he was innocent of Alfred's death. His son Harold succeeded him as Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, and Godwin's other sons held the earldoms of Northumbria, East Anglia and Kent. Harold became king in 1066 but was overthrown and killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Godwin, Earl of Wessex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its name survived in an earldom: in the eleventh century the earls of Wessex, <b>Godwin</b> and his son Harold, were among the most powerful men in England, and Harold became king in 1066.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sussex, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.95,-0.14&amp;year=1034" aria-label="Sussex on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Godwin, Earl of Wessex"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4992 5771h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4992 5771h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.95,-0.14&amp;year=1034">What was here in 1034? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
